cards.definition:
Equivalent to a standard TM (same accepted languages):

- multi-tape TM (simulate with # separators and marked head cells);
- non-deterministic TM (simulate by breadth-first search over configurations);
- TM with a two-way infinite tape, or with extra tracks;
- a PDA with two stacks, and a queue automaton.

Not equivalent (weaker): finite automata, PDA with one stack, linear bounded automata. Not stronger either: no model that is physically realisable has been shown to compute more (Church-Turing thesis).

L is recursive if some TM halts on every input, accepting exactly the members. L is RE if some TM accepts exactly the members (it may loop on others).

Facts:

- recursive ⊂ RE, strictly (the halting language is RE and not recursive);
- L recursive ⇒ complement of L recursive (swap accept and reject);
- L and its complement both RE ⇒ L recursive;
- RE is closed under union, intersection, concatenation and star, but not under complement;
- a subset of a recursive language need not be recursive.
